  "markdown": "## TL;DR\n\nI swept the Docs reader and doc tree at 375, 768 and 1280 px with headless WebKit, fixtures only (no live server, no visible browser). The sweep found four real problems on those pages and one class-wide cause behind them. All are fixed, a new headless test pins each one, and the scoped `REPOOS_CHECK_CHANGED=main repoos check` passes. Two problems I found are outside the Docs reader but share the same cause, so I fixed them too.\n\n## Work and rationale\n\nMost of the Stories detail page was already covered: #1237 audits the story side panel and the Stories list at all three widths, and #1238 covers the Context page states and header. So I aimed the sweep at what those tests did not reach: the Docs reader body, the doc tree, keyboard reach and focus.\n\nFindings, and what fixed each:\n\n1. **Inline code ran off the phone screen.** `.md-rendered code` set `white-space: nowrap`, so one 120-character token measured 876 px wide at 375 px. I switched it to `white-space: normal`. Fenced blocks are unaffected: `.md-rendered pre code` already sets `white-space: pre` and the `pre` scrolls on its own.\n2. **Tree rows and Skills rows were not keyboard-reachable.** They were `div`s with click handlers. I made them `<button type=\"button\">` with a scoped reset that keeps the row layout, and added a `:focus-visible` ring. The Skills list row was converted the same way, with its reset and ring in the global `.skill-row` block. A scoped reset there would have out-specified the existing hover and selected backgrounds, so I kept it global. The folder rows also get `aria-expanded`.\n3. **The doc title was squeezed to one letter per line at 375 px.** The header `.spec-head` had no wrap and the title column had no minimum width, so the editor hint next to it took the space. The overflow audit did not catch this, because nothing overflowed. I found it by reading the screenshot. The header now wraps, and the title column takes the free width. The markdown case asserts the title is wider than 160 px at every width.\n4. **The Skills.sh file review printed a literal `\\n`.** It was a template text node, `…</strong>\\n{{ file.contents }}`, so the reader saw backslash-n. I replaced it with `{{ \"\\n\" }}`.\n\n**The class-wide cause.** Several rules read CSS custom properties that nothing declares: `--bg-overlay`, `--bg-accent-soft`, `--bg-elev`, `--surface-1`, `--surface-2`, `--info`, `--info-alpha`, `--red-alpha`, `--focus`, `--yellow` and `--warning`. Each computed to nothing, so hover and selected backgrounds, the new-doc badges and focus outlines silently vanished. The Docs and history surfaces used several of them. I aliased each one in the same `:root` block that #1231 used, to the existing token that matches its intent (for example `--bg-overlay` to `--nav-hover-bg`, `--focus` to `--border-focus`). I did not alias `--chip`, `--tone`, `--x`, `--y`, `--d` or the `--radix-*` values: they are set inline or by the runtime, so they are not broken.\n\nOne last read of an undeclared property was in `ScheduledTasksPanel.vue`: `var(--warn, var(--status-warn))`. `--status-warn` is declared nowhere, so I pointed the fallback at `--amber`.\n\n**Guards so the class does not return.** Two table-free tests:\n- `css-var-declared.test.ts` scans every `.vue`, `.css` and `.ts` source for `var(--name)` with no fallback and asserts each name is declared somewhere (a `--name:` in CSS or a `'--name':` style key). It passes now and fails on any undeclared read.\n- `template-text-newline.test.ts` walks each SFC's template AST and fails on any text node containing a literal backslash-n. I checked it catches the old snippet.\n\n**Headless browser coverage.** `docs-reader-qa-browser.test.ts` mounts the real `ContextView` in jsdom with a seeded docs store. It serializes the markup into headless WebKit with the built global stylesheet and the view's compiled scoped CSS. Each width checks: no element off-viewport, no control clipped by a scroller, no widened document, live internal links, and the title width. It covers a markdown doc with long tokens, tables and code, a plain-text doc with one 200-character line, and a deep tree with long names. A separate case checks that a tree row is a `BUTTON` in jsdom and shows a ring under keyboard focus in WebKit.\n\n## Approaches tried\n\n- **Checking the fixture CSS.** The first run failed at 375 and 768 on inline code, even after I had changed the rule. The fixture loads the built global stylesheet from `dist/`, which predated the edit. I rebuilt with `bun run build:ui` and the test passed. The lesson: global `style.css` changes need a UI rebuild before the browser test means anything. Scoped CSS is compiled from source and does not need one.\n- **Scoped vs global reset for the Skills row.** My first plan put the button reset in ContextView's scoped block. Scoped selectors carry an attribute, so they would have overridden `.skill-row:hover` and `.skill-row.sel`. I moved that reset into the global block instead.\n- **Audit output.** The first overflow failure named only `code at 60..936`, which was not enough to tell inline from fenced code. I added the element's text to the audit message, which made the cause obvious.\n\n## Challenges and blockers\n\n- The task asks for a walk \"at the three widths\" on the app. I walked fixtures, not a logged-in live app: the harness mounts real components with seeded stores, which is the pattern the repo's other QA tests use. I did not run `repoos serve` or request a preview. Visual confirmation is the captured 375 px screenshot, which I read.\n- `repoos check` skipped its unit `tests` step locally (the remote validation runner runs them). So I ran the affected suites myself: `context`, `deployments-context`, `docs-reader`, `editor-hint`, `deep-link`, `keyboard-shortcuts`, and a batch of ten suites that touch the changed components (schedule-panel, debug-check-focus, close-out-dialogs, card-activity-indicator, dialog-overlay-browser, drawer-control-rows-wrap, hotfix-badge, pipeline-view, done-error-card, held-task-ui). All pass: 14 + 111 + 36 + 2 + 43 + 49 + 91 tests in those runs, and the two new guards pass.\n- Filters that matched no file (`repo-history`, `scheduled`) ran nothing and proved nothing. I replaced them with real file names.\n- The first `repoos check` failed `check-fmt` on my new test file. `bun run fmt` fixed it, and the second run passed.\n\n## Key learnings\n\n- Layout bugs can pass an overflow audit. The one-letter title column did not overflow anything. Keep the screenshot in the loop for text-heavy pages.\n- A class-wide fix needs a guard, or the next undefined token comes back. The alias block plus `css-var-declared.test.ts` is the guard. The AST text check is the matching guard for template text.\n- Keep the wording copy honest. The story panel's empty-state line points to \"New story\", which is a real button on the Stories page. I left it as written.\n\n## Generated change summary\n\nBase tree: `284173a90542c243f5f4687a6d508be1328803bc`\nSource tree: `ae2c41255d722d324f1dc2b7f863544fec9e6792`\n\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/ScheduledTasksPanel.vue: +2 / −2\n- M src/ui-app/src/style.css: +28 / −1\n- M src/ui-app/src/views/ContextView.vue: +36 / −7\n- A src/ui-app/tests/css-var-declared.test.ts: +68 / −0\n- A src/ui-app/tests/docs-reader-qa-browser.test.ts: +400 / −0\n- A src/ui-app/tests/template-text-newline.test.ts: +57 / −0"
